  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nLongwave troughing over the Western CONUS, with south to southwest \nflow aloft across the Northern Rockies, will dominate the upcoming \nweek. Shortwaves ejecting from the base of the trough axis and \nlifting north to northeast within the aforementioned flow regime \nwill bring multiple opportunities for showers and thunderstorms to \nSouthwest through North Central Montana, most notably today and \nWednesday when the areal coverage precipitation will be greatest. \nHigh temperatures, with the exception of today when highs will \ngenerally be 5 to 10 degrees above normal, will mostly range from \nnear to below normal. Periods of strong and gusty winds will occur \nover the next week, especially on Wednesday and Thursday within the \nnorth-south orientated valleys of Southwest Montana where frequent \ngusts in excess of 45 mph are likely to occur. - Moldan\n\n- Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nStrong Winds on Wednesday and Thursday...\n\nECMWF EFIs with respect to winds continue to support the potential \nfor an unusual to very unusual wind event, for early September, \nthrough the period along and south of the I-90 corridor; most \nnotably within the Madison River Valley. Southerly H700 winds per \nNAEFS analysis across Southwest Montana are prog'd to increase to \nbetween 30-40kts by 18z Wednesday, and remain so through 00z Friday \nbefore decreasing. These 2 to 2.5 standard deviation wind speeds \naligned with the north-south terrain in Southwest Montana, \nspecifically the Madison River Valley, combined with deep mixing \nbetween 16z to 02z each day will support an ideal scenario for an \nearly season High Wind event. Given that NBM5.0 probabilities within \nthe Madison River for sustained wind speeds to exceed 40 mph range \nbetween a 60-70% chance (Wednesday) and 30-40% chance (Thursday), \nconfidence remains high enough to continue the High Wind Watch with \nthis forecast package. The main reason for not upgrading the High \nWind Watch was uncertainty with respect to Wednesday night's into \nThursday's winds, which at this point in time appear to be slightly \nweaker than during the daylight hours on Wednesday. Additionally, I \nwas unable to find a (6-9 hour) period in which wind speeds and/or \ngusts decrease significantly enough in order to break-up the event \ninto two (2) distinct wind events, thus the watch from 18z Wednesday \nthrough 03z Friday remains in effect for the Madison River Valley.\n\nListed below are the NBM5.0 probabilities for wind gusts to exceed \n35, 45, and 55 mph for select locations across North Central, \nCentral, and Southwest Montana.\n\n                    Wednesday's Wind Gusts Probabilities\nLOCATION               > 35 mph | > 45 mph | > 55 mph\nBrowning                  95%   |    50%   |     5% \nCut Bank                  95%   |    60%   |    15%\nHavre                     80%   |    40%   |     5%\nGreat Falls               90%   |    60%   |    20%\nLewistown                 60%   |    10%   |    <5%\nHelena                    80%   |    45%   |    15%\nBozeman                   90%   |    65%   |    15%\nDillon                    90%   |    70%   |    25%\nEnnis                    100%   |    85%   |    30%\nWest Yellowstone          95%   |    60%   |     0%\n\n\n                     Thursday's Wind Gusts Probabilities\nLOCATION               > 35 mph | > 45 mph | > 55 mph\nBrowning                  90%   |    65%   |    20% \nCut Bank                  90%   |    65%   |    20%\nHavre                     75%   |    35%   |    <5%\nGreat Falls               80%   |    45%   |     5%\nLewistown                 70%   |     5%   |     0%\nHelena                    75%   |    25%   |     0%\nBozeman                   85%   |    50%   |     5%\nDillon                   100%   |    60%   |    10%\nEnnis                     95%   |    70%   |     5%\nWest Yellowstone          85%   |    40%   |     0%\n\nSevere Thunderstorm Risk for Wednesday...\n\nA marginal risk for severe thunderstorms, mainly capable of \nproducing damaging winds, exists on Wednesday along and northwest of \na Helena, to Monarch, to Winifred line. Showers and thunderstorms \nlifting northeast from Idaho and Western Montana are expected to \noverspread the aforementioned area through the afternoon hours, with \nBUFKIT sounding depicting inverted-V's beneath cloudbases of \n10000kft. These high cloud bases combined with already anomalous low \nlevel wind speeds will support the potential for a few severe \nthunderstorm/shower wind gusts across portions of Central and North \nCentral Montana. - Moldan",
